<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haqqi al-Azm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haqqi al-Azm was a Syrian politician from the prominent Azm family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and a supporter of cooperation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He was appointed first governor of the State of Damascus under the French Mandate in 1920, and he later served as Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1932 to 1934, heading a government with limited powers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haqqi-al-azm.jpg" alt="Haqqi al-Azm" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haqqi_al-Azm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haqqi al-Azm was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, then part of Ottoman Syria, in 1864. He was educated at the Lazarist missionary school in Damascus and then at the military academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. He began as a government clerk and rose to inspector-general at the Ministry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">Awqaf</a>, the religious endowments, but lost the post in 1911 when the Committee of Union and Progress, the Turkish nationalist party, took control of the ministry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After failing to win a seat in the Ottoman parliamentary election of 1912, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and helped found the opposition Ottoman Party for Administrative Decentralization. The Committee of Union and Progress read the party's correspondence with French and British agents as proof that it sought to detach the Arabic-speaking provinces from the empire, and in 1913 al-Azm and other leaders were sentenced to death in absentia. He lived in exile in Cairo, where he also headed the local bureau of the Central Syrian Committee, a French-backed body that promoted the separation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he opposed any role for the Hijaz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> the Hijazi revolt. He sided with the French mandate authorities, and as governor of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> he reportedly won friends in the administration by handing out jobs. He narrowly escaped an assassination attempt while visiting Quneitra with the French High Commissioner, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-gouraud">Henri Gouraud</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1932, after the election of Muhammad Ali Bey al-Abid as president, al-Azm was invited to form a cabinet. The nationalist leaders boycotted parliament, and his government met strong opposition from the National Bloc throughout its two years. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1955 at the age of 91.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haqqi al-Azm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state had a local government under a governor, the first being <b>Haqqi al-Azm</b>, with French advisers holding real authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-damascus">State of Damascus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in Second Syrian Republic, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Haqqi al-Azm"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5448 4687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5448 4687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
